   lexicon  : φ = alpha
   grammar  : 3dom-grammar/1.1.0 (N and P frozen; only Σ moves)
   to change the lexicon, edit candidates/phi_alpha.json and re-render. */

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   3DOM grammar — W3C EBNF  —  version: 3dom-grammar/1.1.0
   Executable notation (::= , * + ? , [ranges]). Transliterate into Lark/ANTLR.
   This file and 3dom_grammar.iso.ebnf are TWO NOTATIONS FOR ONE GRAMMAR; they
   define the same language (see CHANGELOG "Language equivalence").

   NORMATIVE LEXICAL CONVENTIONS  (numbered clauses — part of the specification,
   not commentary). A conforming implementation MUST honour L1–L3 and P1.

     (L1) Inter-token whitespace (space, tab, CR, LF) is INSIGNIFICANT and is
          elided by the lexer per the conventional lexer/parser separation. It
          may appear between any two terminals that lie OUTSIDE a quoted string.
          Consequently the pretty-printed multi-line form
              (recolor () {
                $$( '#wheel' )#move( 2 );
              })();
          is in the language, identical to its minified form.

     (L2) Whitespace INSIDE a quoted selector is SIGNIFICANT and is modelled
          explicitly by the `whitespace` non-terminal, because it IS the
          descendant combinator. Therefore  $$('#car #wheel')  (descendant) and
          $$('#car#wheel')  (compound AND) are DIFFERENT programs with different
          parses. The conformance suite asserts this (coverage.py, gate G5).

     (L3) 3DOM is parsed in TWO LEVELS. The OUTER parser treats a quoted selector
          as a single opaque terminal and elides layout per L1. The INNER parser
          runs over that selector's contents and elides nothing per L2. The
          `quoted_selector` production is the seam: its body is the `selector`
          non-terminal, lexed under L2; every other quoted string (`quoted_string`)
          is an opaque argument value lexed under neither level's selector rules.

     (P1) REFERENCE PARSER = EARLEY. This grammar is intentionally NOT LL(1): on
          seeing whitespace inside a selector the parser cannot choose
          descendant_combinator vs child_combinator without a second token, so
          k = 2 (see METRICS.md, an INVARIANT). Earley is mandated because inputs
          are short (parse cost is irrelevant) and Earley (a) handles k > 1
          directly and (b) reports ambiguity explicitly, which the suite uses as a
          regression test (gate: zero ambiguous parses over the positive corpus).
          A left-factored LL(1) variant is given NON-NORMATIVELY in Appendix A.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

program            ::= iife                                   /* whole output = one IIFE */

iife               ::= '(' 'recolor' '(' ')' '{' statement* '}' ')' '(' ')' ';'
                                                              /* mandatory wrapper; statement* = 0+ lines */

statement          ::= chain_expression ';'                  /* one line, ';'-terminated */

/* ── Core: selector + unbounded operation chain ───────────────────────────── */

chain_expression   ::= selector_call operation_call*         /* '*' = the infinite-chaining rule */

selector_call      ::= '$$' '(' quoted_selector ')'          /* the sole entry point */

quoted_selector    ::= "'" selector "'" | '"' selector '"'   /* D2: quotes agree structurally */

operation_call     ::= '#' verb '(' argument_list? ')'       /* '#'-chained verb; '?' = args optional */

argument_list      ::= argument ( ',' argument )*            /* 1+ comma-separated args */

argument           ::= number | quoted_string               /* D3: no hex_color; typing lives in the IR */

quoted_string      ::= "'" sq_char* "'" | '"' dq_char* '"'   /* D2: quotes agree structurally */

/* ── Selectors: CSS subset ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

selector           ::= pseudo_selector | complex_selector    /* live-selection pseudo, else a graph query */

pseudo_selector    ::= '>' ( 'lasso' | 'function' )          /* :selected / :lasso — resolved off the editor */

complex_selector   ::= compound_selector ( combinator compound_selector )*
                                                              /* compounds joined by combinators */

combinator         ::= child_combinator | descendant_combinator
                                                              /* child listed first (see P1 / k=2) */

descendant_combinator ::= whitespace                         /* bare space = descendant ('#car #wheel') */

child_combinator   ::= whitespace? '*' whitespace?           /* '*' = direct child; padding optional */

compound_selector  ::= wildcard | simple_matcher+            /* '+' = 1+ matchers ANDed, no space */

simple_matcher     ::= id_selector | class_selector | type_selector

id_selector        ::= ':' identifier                        /* #id → userData.label / name */

class_selector     ::= '#' identifier                        /* .class → derived/custom class */

type_selector      ::= 'group' | 'light' | 'camera' | 'selected' /* bare keyword → three.js kind (closed set) */

wildcard           ::= '.'                                   /* '.' → every node in scope */

/* ── Operations: the closed verb set (EXACTLY 15 — a hard invariant) ────────── */

verb               ::= 'scale' | 'move' | 'rotate' | 'delete' | 'spin' | 'duplicate'
                     | 'setMaterial' | 'setOpacity' | 'setVisible' | 'wireframe'
                     | 'metalness' | 'roughness' | 'castShadow' | 'receiveShadow'
                     | 'mesh'

/* ── Lexical primitives ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

number             ::= sign? digit+ ( '.' digit+ )?          /* int or float, optional sign */

sign               ::= '+' | '-'                             /* the '+' / '-' here are QUOTED literals */

identifier         ::= ident_char+                           /* class/id/label name */

ident_char         ::= [a-zA-Z0-9_-]                         /* hyphen allows kebab-case (dump-bed) */

digit              ::= [0-9]                                 /* decimal digit */

whitespace         ::= ' '+                                  /* 1+ spaces — SIGNIFICANT inside selectors (L2) */

sq_char            ::= [^']                                  /* any char except the closing single quote */

dq_char            ::= [^"]                                  /* any char except the closing double quote */


/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   APPENDIX A — NON-NORMATIVE LL(1) variant of the combinator (D4)

   The normative grammar above is k=2 by design and is parsed with Earley (P1).
   IF a project instead needs an LL(1)/LALR front end, the combinator may be
   left-factored as below. It accepts EXACTLY the same language; it is offered so
   the option is explicit and auditable. IMPORTANT: if this variant is ever
   ADOPTED as normative, the "Alien Syntax" grammar MUST be left-factored
   IDENTICALLY — the isomorphism holds over the FINAL grammars, not over drafts.

     complex_selector   ::= compound_selector ( combinator_lf compound_selector )*
     combinator_lf      ::= whitespace ( '>' whitespace? )?   | '>' whitespace?

   Reading: a leading whitespace may be a bare descendant, OR a child whose '>'
   (with optional trailing whitespace) follows; with no leading whitespace it is a
   child. This removes the k=2 first-set overlap on `whitespace` between
   descendant_combinator and child_combinator, yielding an LL(1) decision.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
